	Deconstructing Cognitive Load: Translating Academic Architecture into Investor-Grade Clarity
A foundational principle taught by Y Combinator Group Partner Michael Seibel is that pitching customers and pitching investors require opposite linguistic strategies because the two audiences have fundamentally different motivations4. When speaking with a Search and Rescue incident commander or a government procurement officer, using domain-specific jargon—such as 2 C.F.R. § 200.334 retention protocols, OME-Zarr chunking, or kinematic saturation contours—builds immediate authority and signals insider competence1. Conversely, when pitching generalist venture investors at Y Combinator, industry and academic jargon actively damages the pitch4. Investors evaluate hundreds of companies across disparate verticals; when confronted with dense terminology or business-school abstractions like "The Push of Struggle (  )" and "Service-Dominant Resource Integration," they cannot form a mental picture of how the product works or why customers buy it1.
Kevin Hale codifies this cognitive threshold into three design mandates: slides must be legible, simple, and obvious3. Etymologically, "simple" and "complex" share the Latin root plex, meaning to braid, twist, or fold7. A complex slide braids multiple ideas together, forcing the audience to untangle the narrative while the founder is speaking7. A simple slide contains one fold—a single, unbraided idea stated as a declarative headline in large, high-contrast type positioned at the top of the frame so it can be read effortlessly from the back row of a 500-person room7. Furthermore, Hale emphasizes that an investor pitch must be reproducible: upon hearing the description, a partner should be able to visualize the product immediately and repeat the core thesis to another partner in two sentences without losing fidelity3.
  

When AberTech's technical architecture is unbraided, the underlying commercial logic is exceptionally strong1. Most modern artificial intelligence startups in public safety or drone analytics rely on cloud GPU clusters to process video and satellite feeds1. However, wilderness search and rescue—and tactical defense operations—occur in cellular blackspots where cloud connectivity does not exist, and where streaming gigabytes of uncompressed 1440p drone and dashcam video over satellite links is physically impossible or prohibitively expensive1. Furthermore, when ungrounded AI models run raw object detection over vast wilderness areas, they generate high false-alarm rates (expressed in the current deck as Negative Value of Information,   ), sending exhausted ground teams and $10,000-per-hour helicopters chasing rocks, tree stumps, or livestock1.
AberTech solves both bottlenecks simultaneously through a two-part architectural breakthrough that unites spatio-temporal kinematic bounding with zero-cloud edge telemetry and computer vision1. Before running computer vision on a single pixel, AberTech's pure Kotlin engine (SR Portal) bounds where the missing person can physically be1. By pairing the last known location pin with the subject's mobility mode, elapsed time (  ), and terrain weighting across forest (1.1) and moorland (1.0) using an on-foot saturation model (  ), the system generates offline Uber H3 hexagonal probability rings across 50% (  ), 75% (  ), and 95% (  ) contours1. Simultaneously, it executes automated Go/No-Go equipment rules for UAV wind thresholds, cloud ceilings, and dog scent-age limits (  ) while sizing 4-to-120 searcher allocations with 5-second offline fallback polling, thereby eliminating the 70.4% resource overstatement error common to unbounded searches1. Instead of requiring a $150,000+ enterprise Computer-Aided Dispatch (CAD) system or expensive cloud GPUs, AberTech's three C++17 and OpenCL engines run entirely on an agency's existing legacy laptops in the back of a command vehicle1. Within this unified stack, CompareCompress performs lossless RAM decoding on legacy laptops; Cloud-DashCam streams frame-exact 1440p video and GPX coordinates via lazy OME-Zarr chunk lookups in SQLite (eo_scenes and video_index) without loading entire gigabyte files into memory; and Vision Toolkit uses Fourier wave pre-filtering and Gilbert-Johnson-Keerthi (GJK) polygon pruning on dual-core CPUs and OpenCL to eliminate 60% sensor drift and pinpoint targets across satellite radar, UAV, and dashcam feeds at a variable compute cost of less than $5 per hour1.


