	Reframing Market Architecture: From Constrained Regional Parks to Venture-Scale Public Safety & Tactical Edge
Resolving the UK Uplands Macro Paradox
Slide 2 of the current presentation ("Macro Catalyst // Bannau Brycheiniog & Scottish Highlands 2019–2025") presents a set of localized statistics: countryside day-trip spend grew 51% in 2025, outdoor leisure jumped 30% across 83.9 million Scottish day visits while Merthyr Tydfil peaks surged 51%, and 88% of Brecon Beacons day-trippers (3.41 million visits) spent £14 per day while straining trails1. Alongside this demand surge, Brecon suffered a 17.1% drop in direct tourism full-time equivalent (FTE) staff with a frozen £3.11M core National Park budget, while VisitScotland cut rural infrastructure funding by 67% from £7.9M to £2.6M despite £756M in Highland visitor spend1.
In a venture capital pitch, leading with these specific figures creates a dangerous psychological anchor5. If an investor believes AberTech's primary market consists of UK National Parks whose entire annual infrastructure budgets have been slashed to £2.6M–£3.11M, the company appears uninvestable at venture scale1. Even capturing 100% of UK National Park authorities would yield less than $2M in annual recurring revenue (ARR)1.
To align with Y Combinator's market sizing doctrine, AberTech must separate "Why Now" (the macro scissors effect) from "How Big" (the global bottom-up addressable market)6. The Welsh and Scottish uplands data should be framed strictly as the operational crucible that forced AberTech's architectural breakthrough: post-pandemic wilderness footfall is surging (+30% to +51%) while municipal and park ranger headcount is contracting (-17.1%) and infrastructure budgets are frozen1. Because rescue authorities are trapped in this vise, they can no longer afford to dispatch 20,000 helicopter sorties (150,000 per full alpine rescue) or pay $150,000+ per year for enterprise CAD dispatch suites1. They urgently require a software force multiplier that turns an €85-per-hour drone and a legacy laptop into a 10-minute precision extraction system, collapsing per-mission callout costs to $200–£5001.
  

Bottom-Up and Top-Down Venture Market Sizing
Both Michael Seibel and Kevin Hale emphasize that bottom-up market sizing—multiplying the exact number of target buyers by a realistic annual contract value (ACV)—is far more credible to YC partners than sweeping top-down percentages6. AberTech already possesses the nucleus of a strong bottom-up beachhead in Section 9 of its deck: converting 15 to 30 municipal and national park rescue authorities at $25,000 to 50,000 midpoint ACV) yields its initial $1.0M ARR milestone1.
To demonstrate how that $1.0M ARR beachhead expands into a billion-dollar enterprise, the Market Slide must layer three concentric rings of customer demand supported by verified industry benchmarks1. Within the immediate Serviceable Obtainable Market (SOM) comprising upland, alpine, and rural Search and Rescue authorities across the United Kingdom, European Union, and North America, thousands of mountain rescue teams, national park authorities, sheriff's search-and-rescue divisions, and civil protection agencies manage high-incident wilderness corridors1. Converting just 400 regional and municipal rescue authorities across the UK, EU, and North America at AberTech's $50,000 midpoint ACV represents a $20M ARR initial wedge1. Beyond dedicated mountain rescue units, the core Serviceable Available Market (SAM) encompasses municipal fire, law enforcement, and disaster response agencies across North America and Europe, where search and rescue is predominantly executed by county sheriff departments, municipal fire-rescue services, and state disaster management agencies25. Municipal and local agencies account for 45.95% of the 21.18B Law Enforcement Software market in 2026, while North America anchors 34.1% to 42.3% of global public safety software spend12. Across roughly 18,000 US law enforcement agencies, 29,000 fire departments, and thousands of European civil protection units, capturing 10,000 mid-tier agencies at an average $50,000 annual subscription yields a $500M ARR serviceable market1.
At the macro level, AberTech's Total Addressable Market (TAM) sits at the intersection of three rapidly compounding global software and robotics sectors12. The first pillar is Public Safety Software and Analytics, valued at $13.6B to $17.54B in 2026 and expanding at an 11.2% to 18.4% CAGR to reach $39.4B to $67.72B by 2034–203612. Within this sector, prescriptive analytics—which automates response protocols and unit staging—represents the fastest-growing analytics segment at a 15.4%–16.2% CAGR, while on-premises and edge deployments retain a commanding 37.2% to 58%+ market share due to strict evidentiary custody and data sovereignty mandates12. The second pillar is Commercial and Tactical Drone Software, valued at $7.92B to $11.59B in 2025–2026 and growing at a 16.0% to 19.92% CAGR to reach $24.39B by 2030 and $48.75B by 2035, driven by AI-powered geospatial analytics and beyond-visual-line-of-sight (BVLOS) search and rescue26. The third pillar is Search and Rescue (SAR) Equipment and Robotics, where the broader SAR equipment market stands at $99.48B in 2026 (reaching $122.72B by 2030 at a 5.4% CAGR), while the specialized SAR Robotics and autonomous aerial, marine, and ground segment is surging from 31.18B in 2026) to $60.44B by 2030 and $138.28B by 2035 at an 18.0% CAGR, with software and capability-as-a-service accounting for an increasing share of total contract value28.
