	The Regulatory Go-To-Market Unlock: Turning 2 C.F.R. § 200 into Non-Dilutive Customer Financing
One of the sharpest commercial realizations buried in AberTech's current deck is the connection between OIG Clawbacks ($13.5B in FEMA relief flagged for missing 2 C.F.R. § 200 logs) and Turnkey Grant Compliance (enforcing 3-year 2 C.F.R. § 200.334 retention to cut procurement cost overruns by 29%)1. In the current deck, this is presented passively as "Job 1: Govern & Initialize Audit" on Screens 1–2 and as "Overcoming Adoption Anxiety (  )"1.
In a Y Combinator pitch, this mechanic must be weaponized as a Go-To-Market accelerator5. Investors frequently worry that selling software to rural sheriff departments, municipal fire-rescue units, and national parks entails slow, budget-starved procurement cycles44. AberTech neutralizes this objection by demonstrating that its software pays for itself through two reinforcing financial mechanisms1. First, the platform delivers immediate direct operational savings with a 2.3x Benefit-Cost Ratio: saving just two or three helicopter flight hours (10,000/hr) or avoiding a single 150,000 alpine rescue sortie pays for an agency's entire 75,000 annual AberTech subscription on its very first mission1. Second, the platform unlocks federal grant dollars while preventing audit clawbacks1. In the United States, municipal and volunteer fire-rescue and law enforcement agencies rely heavily on federal grant programs—such as FEMA's Assistance to Firefighters Grants (AFG, where individual municipal awards routinely approach $200,000, such as Dearborn's $193,537.44 FY2025 award, and 25% of funds are earmarked across career, combination, and volunteer departments) and the Department of Justice's Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Grant (JAG) program, which funnels $419 million annually into technology upgrades for smaller and rural departments14. Furthermore, under the Disaster Recovery Reform Act (DRRA) and 2 C.F.R. § 200.334, agencies face severe Office of Inspector General (OIG) audit clawbacks ($13.5B flagged) when they cannot produce unalterable 3-year mission and equipment logs1. Because AberTech's SR Portal automatically generates turnkey 3-year DRRA and 2 C.F.R. § 200 audit trails from field telemetry, agencies can fund the software purchase directly out of federal readiness grants while insulating their broader disaster relief reimbursements from federal clawbacks1.
