While this table reflects rigorous corporate-finance modeling typical of European regional development grants, R&D tax credit appraisals, or lower-middle-market private equity buyouts, it is counterproductive in a Y Combinator or Silicon Valley venture pitch for four interconnected structural reasons1. First, early-stage venture capitalists never value startups on the cost to rewrite C++17 lines of code (725K) or Seller's Discretionary Earnings (2.8x SDE—a metric used to value small brick-and-mortar service businesses or local IT consultancies)1. Quoting a 3.0M IP valuation invites predatory dilution or signals unfamiliarity with standard venture financing mechanics1. Second, the table misclassifies AberTech's gross margin at 53.5%+1. Because AberTech's unified platform executes computer vision and kinematic tiling locally on the customer's existing hardware with less than $5 per hour in variable compute and zero cloud GPU hosting costs, its pure software gross margin is 85% to 90%+1. Presenting a 53.5% gross margin implies that nearly half of AberTech's revenue is consumed by manual consulting labor or low-margin hardware reselling, which depresses software valuation multiples1. Third, Table 2 artificially caps the company's Stage 4 exit at 160M Enterprise Value1. Venture funds rely on power-law returns where a single outlier returns the entire fund ($500M to $5B+ exit), meaning that a 160M ceiling disqualifies the company for institutional seed funds1. In reality, comparable public safety and tactical edge software startups achieve far higher valuations: Prepared (YC W20, AI for 911 centers) raised $145.8M across 6 rounds, including an $80M Series C led by General Catalyst in May 2025, prior to its strategic acquisition by Axon47; similarly, Reveal Technology (offline edge AI for drone mapping) raised a $30M Series B led by Ballistic Ventures and secured a $33.6M DoD STRATFI and $9.5M U.S. Army purchase order21. Fourth, custom IP valuations are incompatible with the Y Combinator Standard Deal, which applies uniform terms to every accepted company without negotiation8.
  

Mathematical Modeling of the Y Combinator Deal and 2026 Post-Money SAFE
Introduced by Y Combinator in 2013 and standardized into its Post-Money SAFE structure in 2018, the SAFE is used in approximately 91% of all pre-seed rounds in 2026, with 73% structured as Valuation Cap Only (no discount)17. Unlike convertible notes, a SAFE is not debt, carries no interest rate, and has no maturity date49. Under a post-money SAFE, investor ownership is locked in immediately upon signing according to a single deterministic formula49:
  

Crucially, post-money SAFEs do not dilute one another prior to a priced equity round; every additional SAFE issued before Series A dilutes the existing common shareholders (the founders)17. When AberTech applies to Y Combinator or raises a standalone pre-seed round from angels and seed funds, the financing mechanics operate across two distinct pathways16.
Under the first pathway—accepting the Y Combinator Standard Deal followed by a Post-Demo Day Seed SAFE—Y Combinator invests $500,000 immediately upon acceptance, split across two instruments: $125,000 on a Post-Money SAFE for a fixed 7.0% ownership stake (which implies a $1.785M post-money valuation on that initial tranche) and $375,000 on an Uncapped Post-Money SAFE with a Most Favored Nation (MFN) provision16. The MFN provision stipulates that when AberTech raises its next SAFE round from outside investors after YC, the $375,000 MFN SAFE automatically adopts the valuation cap and terms of those new investors16. According to 2026 market benchmarks from Kruze Consulting, pre-seed and seed rounds under $1M carry a median post-money valuation cap of $8M to $10M, while rounds of $1M to $2.4M carry a typical post-money valuation cap of $15M17. If AberTech raises $1,500,000 at a $10,000,000 Post-Money Valuation Cap following YC, the conversion math is deterministic16:
  

  

  

  

