	Optimizing the YC Application and 10-Minute Partner Interview
While a crisp 10-slide seed deck is essential for angel and institutional fundraising, Y Combinator partners do not review slide decks during the 10-minute interview; founders are explicitly advised not to share their screens or recite a rehearsed slide presentation8. Instead, YC partners evaluate applicants across five core axes articulated by Dalton Caldwell and Garry Tan: Professionalism, Clarity of Thought, Ability to Execute, Self-Awareness, and Trustworthiness8. Most rejected technical founders do not lose because they lack revenue; they lose because their written application answers are muddled with buzzwords, they hedge three different product directions into one answer, or their founder bios read as theoretical rather than citing shipped artifacts8.
Precision Engineering for the Written YC Application
To score in the top tier across Caldwell's five evaluation axes, AberTech must overhaul its written application narrative to foreground customer specificity and shipped engineering artifacts3. On the 50-character company description—where YC rules dictate naming the specific customer segment and tangible outcome rather than abstract platform technology—AberTech should use Offline drone & radar AI for search and rescue (46 characters) or Search & rescue AI: 500km² to 1km² in 10 minutes (48 characters)1. Similarly, for the primary "What is your company going to make?" prompt, the response must pass the two-sentence test without using terms like "synergy," "ecosystem," or "service-dominant resource integration"1. The optimal framing states: "AberTech makes offline command and computer-vision software for search-and-rescue and public safety agencies. It runs on standard field laptops without cell service, fusing satellite radar, drone video, and dashcam telemetry with physics-based movement models to shrink a    blind search area into a    extraction grid in under 10 minutes."1.
In the founder bio sections, where Caldwell's "ability to execute" axis requires specific, verifiable shipped artifacts with live links or publications, the team should foreground the exact production systems from Section 8 of the original deck: publishing the peer-reviewed OME-Zarr spatial chunking architecture in the ICES Journal (February 2026), building and presenting the 2,000-otolith OpenCL computer vision pipeline at ICES 2024, shipping production monitoring patches across the UK commercial fishing fleet for CEFAS, and engineering British Airways AI2 AWS fleet telemetry1. Simultaneously, the application must resolve the governance ambiguity of "The Rockborne 7"1. Listing seven co-founders on a YC application triggers immediate partner skepticism regarding cap table fragmentation, unequal commitment, and decision-making gridlock, as YC strongly favors 2 to 4 core co-founders with equal or near-equal equity splits who are 100% full-time8. AberTech should designate the 2 or 3 primary full-time architects (CEO and CTO/Chief Scientist) as the official Co-Founders with clean, balanced equity ownership, while presenting the remaining specialists from the Rockborne cohort as Founding Engineers on the option pool1. For the "non-computer system hacked" and resourcefulness prompts, where YC looks for concrete stories of high-agency problem solving rather than academic grade appeals, AberTech should highlight two narratives: reverse-engineering the $13.5B FEMA OIG audit clawback bottleneck by hard-coding 3-year 2 C.F.R. § 200.334 retention directly into the 10-minute tactical triage loop so the software unlocks its own grant funding, and physically hauling laptops and sensors into Wales's Waterfall Country and Iceland snowstorms to calibrate canopy regrowth, uplift turbulence, and map drift firsthand rather than relying on synthetic laboratory datasets1.
